const s=section;
export const articles:Article[]=[
article('houseplant-care-for-beginners','Houseplant care for beginners: a first-year routine that actually works','plant-care','A grower’s starter plan for new plant owners: match plants to measured light, water by drying depth, keep the nursery pot, and hold off feeding until roots settle.','Pick the plant for the light you already have, then water only when the mix has dried to that species’ depth — for most tropical foliage, the top 2–5 cm (1–2 in).',[
s('Light decides what will thrive, so start there','Nearly every beginner failure traces back to a plant placed in less light than it needs. Your eyes adapt to dim rooms; plants cannot. A typical living room away from the windows gets only 50–500 lux, while a spot close to a bright window can reach 10,000–20,000 lux of bright indirect light. Light also falls away fast: a plant 2 m (6 ft) back from the glass often receives only 10–20% of what reaches the sill.\n\nSo choose the spot first and the plant second. From where the leaves will sit, look toward the window: a wide patch of sky means usable light; a wall or ceiling means dim.', ['Low light (500–2,500 lux): ZZ plant (Zamioculcas zamiifolia), snake plant (Dracaena trifasciata), green pothos (Epipremnum aureum ‘Jade’) — they survive here but grow slowly.','Medium light (2,500–10,000 lux): heartleaf philodendron (Philodendron hederaceum), peace lily (Spathiphyllum), spider plant (Chlorophytum comosum).','Bright indirect (10,000–20,000 lux): monstera (Monstera deliciosa), rubber plant (Ficus elastica), variegated pothos such as ‘Marble Queen’.','Some direct sun: aloe (Aloe vera) and most cacti need at least 4–6 hours of bright light.']),
s('Keep the nursery pot and use a cachepot','Leave a new plant in its plastic nursery pot for the first season and drop it inside a decorative outer pot (a cachepot). The nursery pot has drainage holes; most ceramic cachepots do not. At watering time, lift the plant out, water it at the sink until 10–20% of the water runs out the bottom, let it drain, and set it back. Any water that collects in the cachepot should be tipped out within 15–30 minutes, because roots sitting in a puddle run short of oxygen within hours.\n\nResist repotting on day one. The plant is already coping with a move from greenhouse humidity and light to your home, and fresh roots need weeks to settle. Repot next spring if roots are circling or the pot dries out in two or three days.'),
s('Water by drying depth, not by day of the week','Push a finger or a wooden skewer into the mix and water when it has dried to the right depth for that plant. For monstera, pothos, philodendron and rubber plant, that is the top 2–5 cm (roughly the top quarter of the pot). Peace lily wants water sooner, when only the top 1–2 cm is dry. Snake plant, ZZ and aloe should be dry through most of the pot; in winter that can mean one watering every 3–6 weeks.\n\nLift the pot straight after watering and again when it is dry. Within a few weeks you will judge moisture by weight alone, which is faster and more reliable than any calendar.'),
s('Stable temperatures and a two-week quarantine','Most tropical houseplants grow best at 18–27 °C (65–80 °F) and start to suffer below 12–15 °C, so keep them away from winter drafts, exterior doors and the direct blast of heaters or air conditioners. A few yellow or dropped leaves in the first month, especially on figs, are normal adjustment to lower light.\n\nKeep every new arrival apart from your other plants for 2–4 weeks and check leaf undersides and stem joints weekly with a 10× hand lens; spider mites, mealybugs and thrips often arrive on new plants. If you have cats or dogs, note that pothos, monstera, peace lily, ZZ, snake plant and philodendron are all listed as toxic by the ASPCA; spider plant is a safe choice.'),
s('Feed later, and watch the plant every week','Nursery plants usually arrive in mix that already contains fertilizer, often coated slow-release beads, the small round pellets you may see on the surface. Wait until the plant is producing new leaves in your home, usually after 1–2 months, then feed during spring and summer every 2–4 weeks at the label rate, or at half strength if you prefer to feed more often. Cut back or stop in winter, when low light limits growth.\n\nA five-minute weekly walk-round does more than any product: check moisture, look under a few leaves, and give each pot a quarter turn so it grows evenly. PlantPal reminders are a useful prompt for that check, as long as the check, not the date, decides whether you water.')
],['how-to-water-houseplants','bright-indirect-light','potting-mix-vs-garden-soil']),
article('how-to-water-houseplants','How to water houseplants: read the root ball, not the calendar','plant-care','When and how to water indoor plants: species drying depths, the 10–20% runoff rule, fixing water-repellent mix, and why tap water quality matters for sensitive plants.','Water when the mix has dried to the depth that species tolerates, then soak the whole root ball until 10–20% drains out and empty the saucer within 30 minutes.',[
s('Why a fixed schedule drowns plants in winter','Water leaves a pot two ways: roots draw it up and the leaves transpire it, and the surface and walls evaporate it. Both rates rise with light, warmth and air movement. The same monstera that drinks a litre every 7 days in bright July light may take 14–20 days to use the same amount in December, when days are short and growth has slowed. A calendar that ignores this keeps the root zone saturated for weeks.\n\nThat matters because roots breathe. Waterlogged mix runs short of oxygen within hours to days, root tips die first, and root-rot organisms such as Pythium move into the dead tissue. Most “overwatering” is really watering too often, before the mix has had time to let air back in.'),
s('Four ways to check moisture, from quick to precise','Use more than one of these, especially on large pots where the surface dries days before the core. Surface crust is a poor guide: a 30 cm pot can be dust-dry on top and wet 10 cm down.', ['Finger test: push in to the second knuckle, about 5 cm (2 in). Cool and slightly tacky means moisture remains.','Wooden skewer or chopstick: leave it in for a minute; dark, damp particles clinging to it mean wait.','Pot weight: lift right after watering and again when dry. After a few cycles the difference is obvious, even for 10 L pots.','Moisture meter: most cheap probes measure electrical conductivity, so fertilizer salts read as “wet” and coarse bark reads as “dry”. Use them for comparison, not as the final word.']),
s('Match the drying depth to the plant','Tropical foliage plants with thin roots, including monstera, pothos, philodendron, rubber plant and fiddle-leaf fig (Ficus lyrata), should be watered when the top 2–5 cm (roughly the top quarter to third of the pot) is dry. Peace lily is thirstier: water when only the top 1–2 cm has dried, before it collapses, because repeated wilting leaves brown edges. Spider plant sits in between at 2–3 cm.\n\nStorage specialists are the opposite. Snake plant and aloe use CAM photosynthesis, opening their stomata at night to save water, and ZZ stores water in potato-like rhizomes. Let all three dry through 75–100% of the pot. In a cool winter room that can mean 3–6 weeks between waterings.'),
s('How to water: thoroughly, then drain','Use room-temperature water; very cold water can chill tropical roots. Pour slowly over the whole surface, pausing to let it soak in, until 10–20% of the volume runs from the drainage holes. That runoff flushes out excess fertilizer salts and draws fresh air into the mix as the water drains. Empty the saucer or cachepot within 15–30 minutes.\n\nIf water races down the inside of the pot and out the bottom in seconds, the root ball has shrunk and turned water-repellent; peat and coir both do this when bone dry. Stand the pot in a basin of water for 10–30 minutes until the surface darkens, then lift it out and let it drain fully. After that, normal top watering will work again.'),
s('Mind the water itself','Hard tap water leaves a white carbonate crust on the pot rim and surface; it looks untidy but is rarely harmful if you water to runoff. Water from a salt-based softener is a real problem, because it swaps calcium for sodium, which builds up in the mix. Use an unsoftened tap or rainwater instead.\n\nA few plants react to fluoride and boron in some municipal supplies with brown leaf tips: Dracaena, spider plant and peace lily are the classic examples. For those, rainwater or distilled water is worth the effort. PlantPal watering reminders help here too, as long as the reminder is only the prompt to check and the mix decides whether you water.')
],['overwatering-vs-underwatering','bottom-watering-houseplants','root-rot-houseplants']),
article('bright-indirect-light','What is bright indirect light? Numbers, windows and where to put plants','plant-care','Bright indirect light means roughly 10,000–20,000 lux without a sunbeam on the leaves. Learn how to find it by window direction, distance and season in your home.','Bright indirect light is roughly 10,000–20,000 lux at leaf height with no sustained sunbeam on the foliage — usually within about 1 m of a bright window, not across the room.',[
s('Bright indirect light in numbers','The phrase on the plant tag describes a specific band of intensity. Measured at leaf height around midday, bright indirect light is about 10,000–20,000 lux: a wide view of open sky, but no hard-edged patch of sun sitting on the leaves for hours. Hold your hand 30 cm above the leaf. A soft, blurred shadow means indirect light; a crisp, dark shadow means direct sun.\n\nFor comparison, here are the approximate midday ranges growers work with:', ['Typical room away from windows: 50–500 lux (too dim for most plants to grow).','Low light: 500–2,500 lux.','Medium light: 2,500–10,000 lux.','Bright indirect: 10,000–20,000 lux.','Direct sun through glass: 20,000–50,000+ lux.','Full outdoor summer sun: about 100,000 lux.']),
s('Window direction and distance','In the Northern Hemisphere, south-facing windows deliver the most light, north-facing the least; east windows give gentle morning sun that most foliage plants tolerate well, and west windows bring hotter afternoon sun. Swap north and south in the Southern Hemisphere. A balcony overhang, a tree or a building across the street can turn a south window into a medium-light spot.\n\nDistance matters more than most people expect. Light drops steeply as you move back from the glass, and a plant 2 m (6 ft) from a window often receives only 10–20% of what it gets on the sill. In practice, bright indirect light is usually found within about 1 m of an east or north window, or 1–2 m back from, or beside, a south or west window, where the sunbeam misses the leaves. A sheer curtain on a sunny window diffuses the beam and can turn direct sun into bright indirect light.'),
s('Why one reading is not the whole story','Plants respond to the total light they collect over a day, the daily light integral (DLI, in mol/m²/day), not to one snapshot. Six hours at 12,000 lux beats one hour at 30,000 lux followed by gloom. Lux is also weighted to human vision; plant scientists measure photosynthetically active light as PPFD (µmol/m²/s). For sunlight, 1 µmol/m²/s is roughly 54 lux, but grow lights and other sources have different spectra, so there is no universal conversion.\n\nThe practical fix is to take several readings: morning, midday and afternoon, at leaf height, with the sensor facing the window. PlantPal’s light meter is handy for comparing spots this way; a phone sensor is not a lab instrument, but it reliably tells a 1,500-lux shelf from a 15,000-lux one. Repeat in winter, when intensity and day length can both fall by half or more at higher latitudes.'),
s('Plants that need it, and how they tell you','Monstera deliciosa, fiddle-leaf fig, rubber plant cultivars such as ‘Tineke’ and ‘Ruby’, and heavily variegated pothos like ‘Marble Queen’ all need bright indirect light to grow well. In less light, monstera keeps producing small leaves without splits, variegated plants turn greener, and stems stretch with long gaps between leaves (etiolation) as the plant leans toward the window.\n\nToo much sun shows differently: bleached, pale patches or crisp brown areas on the leaves facing the glass, often appearing within days of a move. Aloe and most succulents are the exception on this page: they want direct sun, at least 4–6 hours of bright light.'),
s('Moving a plant into brighter light','Leaves built in shade have thinner tissue and fewer protective pigments, so a sudden move into strong light can scorch them. Step the change up over 1–2 weeks: start with a spot that gets a few hours of morning sun, then move closer to the window. Keep watering by drying depth; a plant in brighter light uses water faster, so expect to water more often.\n\nRecheck placements twice a year. Summer sun can scorch a plant that was perfectly placed in March, and the same spot may be too dim by November.')
],['low-light-houseplants','using-a-plant-light-meter','houseplant-care-for-beginners']),
article('potting-mix-vs-garden-soil','Potting mix vs. garden soil: why ground soil fails in a pot','soil-fertilizer','Garden soil compacts and stays waterlogged in containers. See how potting mix is engineered for air and water, what to read on the bag, and a blend for each plant.','Never fill a pot with garden soil: in a shallow container it compacts, holds a saturated layer at the base and starves roots of air. Use a container mix and adjust it for the plant.',[
s('The physics of a pot: the perched water table','In a garden bed, water drains away because there is a deep column of soil beneath the roots, full of earthworm channels and fungal networks that keep it open. A pot cuts that column short. When water reaches the bottom of a container it cannot pull free into the soil below, so a saturated layer sits at the base: the perched water table. The shorter the pot and the finer the material, the thicker that soggy layer becomes.\n\nGarden soil is dominated by fine silt and clay particles. In a bed they form stable crumbs; in a pot, repeated watering breaks the crumbs apart, the particles settle, and within a few months the soil sets into a dense block with almost no air space. Roots in that block run short of oxygen, root tips die, and root-rot organisms move in.'),
s('Why a gravel layer makes it worse','Putting stones or broken pots in the bottom “for drainage” is one of the most persistent myths in gardening. Water does not flow easily from a fine material into a coarse one, so it stays in the soil above until that layer is fully saturated. A gravel layer therefore raises the perched water table higher into the root zone, rather than removing it, and it takes up room the roots could use.\n\nIf a decorative pot has no hole, the fix is to grow the plant in a nursery pot with drainage holes and stand it inside, emptying any collected water after each watering.'),
s('What potting mix is actually made of','Most bagged potting mixes contain no field soil at all. They are soilless blends built for air and water in a shallow container. A typical indoor mix uses peat or coir as the water-holding base, perlite, pumice or bark for coarse pores that stay open, lime to raise the pH of peat from about 3.5–4.5 to a plant-friendly 5.5–6.5, a wetting agent so dry mix accepts water, and often a starter fertilizer. Because the ingredients are processed and often heat-treated, they are also largely free of the weed seeds, slugs, root-knot nematodes and soil-borne diseases that garden soil can carry indoors.\n\nCompost is valuable in the ground but a poor stand-alone container medium: it is fine-textured, variable in salt content and breaks down quickly. Keep it to a small share of a blend, if you use it at all.', ['Check the intended use: “indoor” or “container” on the label, not “garden soil” or “topsoil”.','Read the ingredient list, not the product name; “potting soil” and “potting mix” are used interchangeably.','Look for a fertilizer claim such as “feeds up to 6 months” so you do not add feed on top of it.','Squeeze a damp handful: it should crumble apart, not form a sticky ball.']),
s('Tuning a bagged mix for the plant','A standard indoor mix suits peace lily, spider plant, ferns and most seedlings as it comes. Aroids that grow as climbers in the wild, such as monstera, philodendron and pothos, root better in an open blend: a reasonable starting point is 1 part potting mix, 1 part fine orchid bark (6–20 mm chunks) and 1 part perlite or pumice by volume. Succulents and cacti want 40–60% mineral grit such as pumice or perlite so the mix dries within days.\n\nPot and light change the answer as much as the recipe. The same blend that suits a monstera in a bright window can stay wet for weeks in a dim corner or an oversized pot, so adjust the mix and the pot together and watch how quickly the first few waterings dry out.')
],['perlite-vs-pumice','repotting-houseplants','coco-coir-vs-peat']),
article('overwatering-vs-underwatering','Overwatering vs. underwatering: how to tell two wilts apart','troubleshooting','A wilting plant can be bone dry or drowning. Use pot weight, moisture 5 cm down, smell and leaf texture to diagnose it in a minute and apply the right fix.','Never water a wilting plant until you have checked the mix 5 cm (2 in) down: dry mix plus limp leaves means thirst, wet mix plus limp leaves means the roots are failing.',[
s('Why both problems look the same','A leaf stays firm because its cells are full of water pressing outward (turgor). When the roots cannot deliver enough water, the leaf goes limp, whatever the reason. In a dry pot there is simply no water to take up. In a waterlogged pot there is plenty of water, but the roots have been starved of oxygen, the fine root tips that do most of the absorbing have died, and the plant is in effect in drought while standing in a puddle.\n\nThat is why the reflex of watering every drooping plant is dangerous. For the dry plant it is the cure; for the drowning one it speeds root death. The leaves cannot tell you which is which. The pot can.'),
s('The one-minute diagnosis','Run through these checks before you pick up the watering can. Two or three pointing the same way are usually conclusive.', ['Pot weight: noticeably light suggests dry; heavy suggests wet.','Moisture 5 cm (2 in) down: dusty and pulled away from the pot wall, or cool and soggy?','Smell: a sour, rotten or swampy smell from the mix points to waterlogging and rot.','Leaf texture: thin, papery, curled or crisp suggests thirst; soft, yellowing and limp suggests wet roots.','Which leaves: sudden yellowing of several lower leaves at once is more typical of a wet root zone than of drought.','Stem base: soft, dark or collapsing tissue at soil level is a wet-root sign on snake plant, ZZ and succulents.']),
s('Fixing a plant that is too dry','A peace lily (Spathiphyllum) is the textbook case: it collapses dramatically when the mix dries and recovers within a few hours of watering. Most tropical foliage behaves similarly if caught early. Water slowly until 10–20% runs out the bottom; if water shoots straight through a shrunken root ball, soak the pot in a basin for 10–30 minutes until the surface darkens, then drain it fully.\n\nLeaves that went crisp will not recover, and repeated wilting leaves brown margins, especially on peace lily. If the plant needs water every two or three days, look at the cause: roots filling the pot, a small terracotta pot in a sunny window, or a very coarse mix. Moving up one pot size in spring usually solves it.'),
s('Fixing a plant that is too wet','Stop watering. Lift the plant out of any cachepot and tip out trapped water; blocked drainage holes and hidden puddles cause much of this problem. Move the plant to brighter indirect light (not harsh sun) so it uses more water, and let the mix dry to the right depth for the species before watering again.\n\nIf the plant is still declining after a week, or the mix smells sour, slide it out of the pot and look at the roots. Firm, pale or tan roots mean it will recover once the mix dries. Brown, mushy roots whose outer layer slides off at a touch mean root rot, and the rescue steps in the root-rot guide apply. Overwatering is almost always about frequency, not the size of one watering: a thorough soak followed by a proper dry-down is healthy.'),
s('Other causes that mimic watering problems','Cold is the great impostor. A tropical plant chilled below about 12 °C (54 °F) by a drafty window or a car ride home wilts and yellows even in perfectly moist mix, and figs respond by dropping leaves. Fresh repotting, root damage, a sudden move into strong sun and heavy spider mite infestations can all produce limp or yellowing foliage too; turn leaves over and inspect with a 10× lens before blaming the water.\n\nChange one thing at a time and give it 1–2 weeks. Recovery shows first in new growth, not in the leaves that are already damaged.')
],['how-to-water-houseplants','root-rot-houseplants','yellow-leaves-houseplants']),
article('repotting-houseplants','Repotting houseplants: when to do it, what size pot and how','plant-care','Repot only for a real reason, in spring, into a pot 2–5 cm wider. A grower’s guide to the signs, pot sizing, root handling and the aftercare that prevents root rot.','Repot in spring when roots have filled the pot, and go up only 2–5 cm (1–2 in) in diameter; an oversized pot surrounds the roots with wet mix they cannot use.',[
s('Signs a plant really needs a new pot','Most actively growing foliage plants need repotting every 1–3 years, but the plant, not the calendar, should tell you. Slide it out of its pot when the mix is slightly dry and look at the root ball. Roots wound in dense circles around the outside, with little mix visible, mean it is ready. So does water that runs straight through without wetting the mix, a pot that needs watering every 2–3 days in spring, or a plant so top-heavy it keeps tipping over.\n\nThe mix itself also wears out. Bark and peat break down over 2–3 years into fine, soggy material that holds less air, and a white crust of fertilizer salts builds up on the surface. Refreshing the mix is a valid reason to repot even when the pot size stays the same.'),
s('Reasons not to repot yet','A single root poking out of a drainage hole is not enough on its own; roots explore any opening. Avoid repotting a plant you have just bought, since it is already adjusting to your light and humidity, and avoid late autumn and winter, when low light means roots regrow slowly and fresh, wet mix stays wet. Spring to early summer, when the plant is putting out new leaves, is when root damage heals fastest.\n\nSome plants prefer snug pots. Snake plant (Dracaena trifasciata), ZZ (Zamioculcas zamiifolia) and spider plant store water in rhizomes or tuberous roots and are prone to rot in oversized pots; peace lilies also tend to flower better when slightly root-bound. For these, wait until the roots start to deform the pot.'),
s('Choosing the next pot','Go up 2–5 cm (1–2 in) in diameter for small and medium plants and up to 5–8 cm for large floor specimens. The reason is water, not space: mix the roots do not reach stays wet far longer than mix full of roots, and that unused wet zone is where rot starts. Always use a pot with drainage holes.\n\nTerracotta loses water through its walls and dries noticeably faster than plastic, which suits succulents, snake plant and heavy-handed waterers. Plastic and glazed ceramic hold moisture longer, which suits peace lily, ferns and anyone who tends to forget watering.'),
s('Step by step','Water the plant a day ahead so the root ball holds together and the roots are hydrated, then work through these steps:', ['Ease the plant out by tipping the pot and pressing on its sides; cut a stuck plastic pot rather than pulling the stem.','Tease out circling roots with your fingers. A badly matted root ball can be scored with three or four shallow vertical cuts to encourage new outward roots.','Trim dead roots (brown, hollow or mushy) with clean scissors; keep firm, healthy ones.','Set the plant at the same depth it grew before; burying the stem invites rot, especially on snake plant and ZZ.','Fill around the sides with fresh, slightly damp mix, firming lightly with your fingers; do not pack it hard.','Water thoroughly until it drains to settle the mix, then top up any low spots.']),
s('The first four weeks','Keep the plant in bright indirect light but out of direct sun for 1–2 weeks while roots recover. Check moisture by feel, because the new pot and fresh mix will dry on a different schedule from the old one; many repotted plants need less water than expected for the first few weeks. A little leaf drop or droop is normal.\n\nHold off fertilizer for about 4–6 weeks. Most fresh mixes contain starter fertilizer, and newly cut roots are vulnerable to salt damage. If you are repotting a monstera or climbing philodendron, add a moss or coir pole now, while the root ball is out; inserting one later tears roots.')
],['potting-mix-vs-garden-soil','root-rot-houseplants','terracotta-vs-plastic-pots']),
article('fertilizing-houseplants','Fertilizing houseplants: how much, how often and when to stop','soil-fertilizer','Feed houseplants every 2–4 weeks in spring and summer, pause in low winter light, and never feed dry or damaged roots. A grower’s guide to dose, timing and warning signs.','Feed only when the plant is actively growing — typically every 2–4 weeks from spring to late summer at label rate — and cut back or stop in winter unless the plant is under grow lights.',[
s('Light drives growth; fertilizer only supplies it','Plants make their own sugars from light. Fertilizer supplies the mineral raw materials, nitrogen for leaves and chlorophyll, potassium for water regulation, magnesium at the heart of each chlorophyll molecule, and so on, but it cannot make up for a shortage of light. A pothos in a 1,000-lux corner grows slowly because it is short of light, not of nitrogen, and feeding it more simply leaves unused salts in the mix.\n\nThat is also why feeding tracks the seasons. In temperate homes, most houseplants grow from roughly spring to late summer and slow sharply as days shorten. A plant under grow lights, or in a very bright winter window, keeps growing and can keep being fed at a lower rate.'),
s('A practical feeding calendar','These intervals assume a liquid fertilizer used at the manufacturer’s label rate; always follow your local label.', ['Early spring: restart when new leaves appear, at half strength for the first feed.','Spring to late summer: every 2–4 weeks at label rate, or half strength every 1–2 weeks if you prefer steadier, smaller doses.','Early autumn: stretch to every 4–6 weeks as growth slows.','Late autumn and winter: stop for most plants in natural light; continue at quarter to half strength only for plants under grow lights that are clearly growing.','After repotting or buying: wait 4–8 weeks, since most fresh and nursery mixes already contain fertilizer.']),
s('Choosing a formula','For foliage plants, a balanced or nitrogen-leaning liquid feed does the job: products such as 1-1-1, 2-2-2 or a 3-1-2 ratio are all typical. A good houseplant fertilizer also lists magnesium, iron, manganese and other micronutrients in its guaranteed analysis; soilless mixes based on peat, coir and bark contain very little of these on their own. High-phosphorus “bloom” formulas are unnecessary for foliage plants.\n\nIf you want a reference point for strength, commercial growers keep the feed solution for most foliage houseplants at an electrical conductivity of roughly 0.8–1.5 mS/cm. An inexpensive EC pen lets you check your mixed solution, which is useful if you use hard tap water that already carries dissolved minerals.'),
s('How to apply it safely','Apply fertilizer solution to mix that is already slightly moist, never to bone-dry mix. Dry roots take up the concentrated salts too quickly and the tips burn. Water to 10–20% runoff as usual, so excess salts are carried out rather than accumulating. Measure with a syringe or measuring spoon rather than guessing by the capful, and do not double the next dose to make up for a missed one.\n\nEvery 3–4 months during the growing season, flush the pot with plain water equal to 2–3 times the pot’s volume to wash out built-up salts; skip this for plants with rotting roots or poor drainage.'),
s('Reading the plant: too little or too much','Genuine hunger shows as slow growth, new leaves smaller than the old, and a uniform paling of the older leaves as the plant moves nitrogen to new growth. Before blaming nutrients, rule out low light and wet roots, which cause the same pale, slow look and are far more common.\n\nOverfeeding shows as a white crust on the mix surface and pot rim, brown leaf tips and margins, and wilting despite moist mix, because concentrated salts pull water out of the roots. If you see those signs, stop feeding, flush with 2–3 pot volumes of clean water if drainage and roots are sound, and resume at half strength once new growth looks normal.')
],['npk-fertilizer-numbers','liquid-vs-slow-release-fertilizer','fertilizer-burn-houseplants']),
article('npk-fertilizer-numbers','NPK numbers explained: what a fertilizer label really tells you','soil-fertilizer','What 10-10-10 or 3-1-2 really means: percent N, P₂O₅ and K₂O by weight, how to convert to elemental nutrients, and why ratio and dilution matter more than size.','NPK numbers are percentages by weight of nitrogen, phosphate (P₂O₅) and potash (K₂O); for houseplants, the ratio and the diluted strength you apply matter far more than how big the numbers are.',[
s('What the three numbers stand for','Every fertilizer label leads with three numbers, always in the same order. The first is total nitrogen (N) as a percentage of the product’s weight. The second is available phosphate, reported as P₂O₅, and the third is soluble potash, reported as K₂O. So a 100 g bag of 20-20-20 contains 20 g of nitrogen, 20 g of P₂O₅ and 20 g of K₂O, and the remaining 40% is the other chemical components of those salts, micronutrients, and in liquids mostly water.\n\nThe oxide convention is a historical quirk from 19th-century soil chemistry, and it matters because the plant does not use P₂O₅ or K₂O; it takes up phosphate and potassium ions.'),
s('Converting to what the plant actually gets','To find the elemental phosphorus, multiply the middle number by 0.436. For elemental potassium, multiply the last number by 0.83. A 20-20-20 is therefore really about 20% N, 8.7% P and 16.6% K. Two products that look identical on the front of the bag can differ once you convert, and it explains why a “balanced” 1-1-1 actually delivers much less phosphorus than nitrogen.\n\nIt also lets you work out strength. Dissolving 1 g of a 20-20-20 powder in 1 litre of water gives 1,000 ppm of product, 20% of which is nitrogen: 200 ppm N. That is a strong greenhouse-level feed; most houseplant labels land well below it, and many interior growers aim for roughly 50–150 ppm N at each feed.'),
s('Ratio versus concentration','A 1-1-1 and a 10-10-10 have the same ratio; the second is simply ten times more concentrated, so you use a tenth as much. Liquid houseplant feeds tend to have small numbers because they are mostly water: per the manufacturers, Miracle-Gro Indoor Plant Food (US) is 1-1-1 and Espoma Organic Indoor! Houseplant Food is 2-2-2. Soluble powders for mixing at home often read 20-20-20 or 24-8-16. None is “stronger” in use once diluted as the label directs, so compare products at their working dilution, not by the digits on the front.\n\nThe ratio is what tells you the fertilizer’s purpose. Foliage plants use more nitrogen than anything else, which is why a 3-1-2 ratio (for example 9-3-6 or 12-4-8) is a common professional choice for green plants.'),
s('The bloom-booster myth and the rest of the label','High-phosphorus “bloom” fertilizers such as 10-30-20 do not make houseplants flower more. Flowering is driven mainly by light, maturity and, for some species, day length or a cool spell; a peace lily in 1,500 lux will not bloom however much phosphorus it gets. Excess phosphorus simply washes out or, in soilless mixes, can interfere with the uptake of iron and zinc.\n\nThe small print below the NPK figures is often more useful than the headline numbers:', ['Nitrogen form: nitrate and ammoniacal N are available immediately; urea must be converted first, more slowly in cool mix.','Secondary nutrients: calcium (Ca), magnesium (Mg) and sulfur (S). Coir-based mixes in particular benefit from added Ca and Mg.','Micronutrients: iron (Fe), manganese (Mn), zinc (Zn), copper (Cu), boron (B) and molybdenum (Mo). Soilless mixes supply very little of these.','Chelated iron (listed as EDTA or DTPA) stays available at the slightly acidic pH of most potting mixes.','Derived from: shows the actual salts or organic sources, useful if you want to avoid urea or check for organic inputs.'])
],['fertilizing-houseplants','liquid-vs-slow-release-fertilizer','fertilizer-burn-houseplants']),
article('perlite-vs-pumice','Perlite vs. pumice: choosing the right mineral for your potting mix','soil-fertilizer','Perlite is light, cheap and floats; pumice is heavier, holds some water in its pores and never breaks down. Compare them for aroids, succulents and large pots.','Use perlite where light weight and low cost matter, and pumice for succulents, top-heavy plants and long-lived pots; either way, choose 3–6 mm particles and screen out the dust.',[
s('Two volcanic rocks, one made in a furnace','Both start as volcanic glass. Perlite is mined as a dense glassy rock, crushed, then flash-heated to about 850–900 °C; the water trapped inside turns to steam and puffs each grain up to 4–20 times its original volume, like popcorn. The result is the familiar white, very light granule with a pH near neutral (about 7), no nutrients and no cation exchange capacity; it is essentially inert.\n\nPumice formed the same way naturally: gas-charged lava froth that cooled so fast its bubbles were frozen in place. It is also inert and nutrient-free, but it is denser, usually grey or cream, and its internal pores are partly connected, so it soaks up and holds a little water inside each grain.'),
s('How they compare in the pot','Both are added for the same reason: rigid particles that do not compress hold open large pores between them, so air returns to the root zone after each watering. The differences show up in weight, water behavior and durability.', ['Weight: perlite is extremely light; pumice is several times heavier, which steadies top-heavy plants.','Water: perlite holds water mostly on its rough surface and in surface crevices; pumice also stores some water in internal pores and releases it slowly.','Floating: perlite floats and migrates to the surface when you water; pumice mostly stays put.','Durability: perlite can crush over years in a dense, compacted mix; pumice does not break down.','Cost and availability: perlite is cheap and sold everywhere; pumice is often pricier and easiest to find through bonsai, cactus or landscape suppliers.']),
s('Where each one earns its place','Perlite is the default in light, general-purpose blends and in hanging baskets or large floor pots you need to move. In an aroid mix for monstera, philodendron or pothos, a starting point of 1 part potting mix, 1 part fine bark and 1 part perlite or pumice by volume works well; either mineral does the job there.\n\nPumice is the better choice for succulents and cacti, where 40–60% of the mix should be mineral grit: it adds weight to anchor tall plants, does not float out of the pot, and its gentle internal water store suits roots that must dry quickly without being parched. It also suits long-lived specimens repotted only every few years, because it keeps its structure while the organic part of the mix breaks down around it.'),
s('Particle size matters as much as material','A bag of fine perlite powder or sandy pumice fines does not open up a mix; it fills the gaps and can make drainage worse. For most houseplant pots, particles of roughly 3–6 mm are the useful range, with slightly larger grades for large pots and slightly smaller ones for pots under 10 cm. Tip a new bag through a kitchen sieve or garden riddle to remove the dust before mixing.\n\nHandle both damp. Dry perlite and pumice produce fine dust that contains respirable silica, so wet the bag before opening or pouring and work outside or wear a dust mask when mixing large volumes. Always buy horticultural grades rather than building or pool-filter products, whose particle sizes and additives are not designed for plants.')
],['potting-mix-vs-garden-soil','aroid-potting-mix','coco-coir-vs-peat']),
article('coco-coir-vs-peat','Coco coir vs. peat moss: how each behaves in your pots','soil-fertilizer','Peat is acidic and holds 10–20× its weight in water; coir is near-neutral but can carry salts. Compare pH, rewetting, nutrition and sustainability for houseplant mixes.','Peat and coir both make good potting-mix bases, but buy coir that is washed and buffered, and feed coir mixes with calcium and magnesium to prevent deficiencies.',[
s('Where each comes from, and the carbon question','Sphagnum peat is partly decomposed moss harvested from bogs, where waterlogged, acidic conditions have preserved it over thousands of years. It has been the backbone of professional potting mixes for decades because it is uniform, light and holds a huge amount of water, about 10–20 times its dry weight. Its cost is environmental: peatlands are among the planet’s largest land carbon stores, and draining and cutting them releases that carbon and destroys specialized habitat. Several countries, including the UK, are phasing peat out of retail compost.\n\nCoir, or coco peat, is the spongy pith left over when fiber is stripped from coconut husks, a by-product of the coconut industry in countries such as Sri Lanka and India. It is renewable, though it is shipped long distances and needs careful processing before it is fit for plants.'),
s('Chemistry: pH, salts and buffering','Raw peat is strongly acidic, pH 3.5–4.5, so commercial mixes add ground limestone to bring it to about 5.5–6.5, the range where most houseplants take up nutrients best. Coir starts much closer to that range, at roughly pH 5.5–6.8, so it needs little or no lime.\n\nCoir’s weakness is salt. Husks are often soaked in brackish water during processing, and unwashed coir can carry high levels of sodium and potassium. Its exchange sites also hold on to potassium and sodium and grab calcium and magnesium from the feed, which can leave plants short of both. Good horticultural coir is therefore washed to remove salts and “buffered”, pre-treated with calcium nitrate so the exchange sites are already loaded with calcium. Look for “washed” and “buffered” on the label.'),
s('Water, air and rewetting','Both hold plenty of water, but they hold it differently. Peat is fine and spongy; it stays wet for a long time and can collapse and compact as it ages over 2–3 years. Coir pith generally drains a little more freely and holds slightly more air at the same moisture level, and it resists breakdown longer. That makes a coir-based mix slightly more forgiving for heavy-handed waterers, although fine, dusty coir can compact as badly as peat.\n\nBoth turn water-repellent when bone dry, peat more stubbornly than coir. If water runs off the surface and down the pot sides, soak the pot in a basin for 10–30 minutes until the surface darkens, then drain it. Some mixes include a wetting agent to reduce this problem.'),
s('Preparing coir bricks','Compressed bricks are the cheapest way to buy coir and expand 5–7 times in volume when wet. A 650 g brick typically makes around 8–9 litres.', ['Use a bucket that holds at least seven times the brick’s volume and add warm water gradually.','Break up the expanding block by hand once it softens, after about 15–30 minutes.','If the label does not say washed or low-EC, rinse it by filling and draining the bucket two or three times.','If it is not buffered, feed with a fertilizer that contains calcium and magnesium from the first watering.','Squeeze a handful: a few drops should appear, not a stream. Too wet? Spread it out to dry a little before mixing.']),
s('Which to use for which plant','For most houseplants either works as the base of the mix; what matters more is the coarse material you add, such as bark and perlite or pumice, and the pot size. Peat-based mixes are forgiving for ferns, peace lilies and calatheas that like steady moisture. Coir suits growers avoiding peat; like any organic base, it discourages fungus gnats best when the top 2–5 cm is allowed to dry between waterings. Per the manufacturer, Miracle-Gro Indoor Potting Mix (US) is one widely sold coir-containing indoor mix.\n\nWhen you switch from one to the other, expect different drying times and check moisture by feel for the first few weeks. With coir, watch for magnesium deficiency, yellowing between the veins of older leaves, and choose a fertilizer that lists Ca and Mg in its guaranteed analysis.')
],['potting-mix-vs-garden-soil','perlite-vs-pumice','how-to-water-houseplants']),
article('liquid-vs-slow-release-fertilizer','Liquid vs. slow-release fertilizer: choosing a feeding system for houseplants','soil-fertilizer','Liquid feed gives precise control; coated slow-release prills feed for 3–9 months but speed up in warm rooms. How each works, when to use it, and why not to stack them.','Choose liquid feed if you want to adjust by season and plant, controlled-release prills if you want one application per season — but never combine both with a pre-fertilized mix.',[
s('How liquid feed works','A liquid concentrate or dissolved soluble powder delivers nutrients already in solution, so roots can take them up within hours. That makes it the most controllable system: you choose the dose and frequency at every watering, you can feed a hungry pothos and skip a resting snake plant on the same day, and you can stop instantly if something goes wrong. Typical use is every 2–4 weeks at label rate through spring and summer, or half strength more often.\n\nThe cost is effort and the risk of measuring errors. Nutrients not taken up are washed out at the next watering, so liquid feed gives a sawtooth supply, high just after feeding and tapering off, which plants handle perfectly well.'),
s('How controlled-release prills work','Controlled-release fertilizers (CRF), such as the resin-coated prills sold under the Osmocote name, are granules of soluble fertilizer sealed inside a polymer or resin shell. Water vapor diffuses in through the coating, dissolves the nutrients, and the solution slowly diffuses back out. The rate depends mainly on temperature, not on how much you water. Label durations such as 3–4, 6 or 8–9 months are measured at about 21 °C (70 °F); in a 26 °C summer room a prill releases faster and runs out sooner, and in a cool room it lasts longer.\n\nWork the prills into the top few centimetres of mix at the label rate rather than leaving them on the surface, and never crush them: a broken coating dumps its entire content at once. Many nursery plants arrive with CRF already in the mix, visible as small round beads.'),
s('Slow-release is not always controlled-release','Labels use “slow-release” loosely. Coated prills release by diffusion and temperature. Organic fertilizers, such as feather meal, bone meal or composted manure pellets, release nutrients only as soil microbes break them down, which is slow and patchy in a sterile, soilless potting mix and in cool winter rooms. Uncoated granules and fertilizer spikes are something else again: they dissolve relatively quickly and create a concentrated hot spot of salts next to the roots, a common cause of localized root burn in small pots.\n\nRead the method on the label, not the word on the front. A soluble granule meant for dissolving in a watering can should never be scattered into a houseplant pot.'),
s('Matching the system to the situation', 'Neither system is better overall; each suits different growers and plants.', ['Mixed collection with different needs: liquid, so each plant gets its own rate.','Large pots, outdoor summer containers or busy owners: controlled-release, one application per season.','Plants going to a plant sitter for weeks: controlled-release avoids asking someone else to measure fertilizer.','Winter in a warm, dim home: liquid, or let the CRF run out, because prills keep releasing in a heated room even when low light has stopped growth.','Salt-sensitive plants such as Dracaena, spider plant and peace lily: liquid at half strength, so you can control exactly what goes in.']),
s('The stacking problem','The most common fertilizer burn in houseplants comes from layering systems: a mix marketed as feeding for months, then a scoop of prills, then weekly liquid feed on top. Per the manufacturer, Miracle-Gro Indoor Potting Mix (US), for example, “feeds up to 6 months”; adding more fertilizer during that period is unnecessary. Check the bag for a feeding claim before you add anything.\n\nA sensible hybrid is to rely on CRF or a pre-fed mix for the first months after potting and switch to diluted liquid feed once that supply is spent. If a white crust or brown leaf tips appear, stop all feeding and flush the pot with 2–3 times its volume of clean water, provided the roots are healthy and the pot drains freely.')
],['fertilizing-houseplants','npk-fertilizer-numbers','common-houseplant-fertilizers']),
article('yellow-leaves-houseplants','Yellow leaves on houseplants: read the pattern to find the cause','troubleshooting','Which leaves yellow, and how, points to the cause: old-leaf turnover, wet roots, low light, cold, pests or nutrients. A pattern-by-pattern diagnostic guide.','One old lower leaf yellowing slowly is normal turnover; several leaves yellowing within a week, or yellow new growth, means something in the root zone or environment has changed.',[
s('Why leaves turn yellow at all','A leaf is green because of chlorophyll, which the plant constantly breaks down and rebuilds. Yellowing (chlorosis) means rebuilding has stopped, and the yellow carotenoid pigments that were there all along show through. Plants do this deliberately to old leaves: as a lower leaf becomes shaded and less productive, the plant dismantles it and moves its mobile nutrients, nitrogen, magnesium and potassium, to new growth.\n\nThat is why the position of the yellowing is the single most useful clue. Nutrients that move within the plant show shortages on old leaves first; nutrients that cannot move, such as iron and calcium, show shortages on new leaves. Stress on the roots usually hits the older, lower leaves first too.'),
s('Pattern guide','Match what you see to the most likely cause, then confirm it by checking the mix and roots.', ['One or two of the oldest, lowest leaves, slowly, with healthy new growth: normal aging. Remove once fully yellow.','Several lower leaves at once, soft, with wet mix: overwatering or root rot; check the roots.','Lower leaves yellow and crisp, with dry mix pulling from the pot wall: underwatering.','Uniform pale green to yellow of older leaves, slow growth, months without feeding: nitrogen shortage.','Yellow between the veins of older leaves, veins stay green: magnesium shortage, common in coir mixes.','Yellow between the veins of new leaves: iron shortage, usually because the mix pH is too high.','Fine pale speckling, dull or bronzed leaves, maybe webbing underneath: spider mites.']),
s('Water and roots come first','In houseplants, a root zone that stays too wet is the most common cause of yellow leaves. Waterlogged mix runs out of oxygen, root tips die, and the plant cannot take up the nitrogen it needs, so the leaves yellow even in a well-fed pot. Lift the pot, check the mix 5 cm (2 in) down, and look for water trapped in a cachepot. ZZ plants show it clearly: stems yellowing from the base in wet mix is an early sign of rhizome rot.\n\nIf the mix has stayed wet for more than a week and yellowing is spreading, slide the plant out and look. Firm roots mean you can simply let the mix dry; mushy brown roots mean root rot needs treating. Do not add fertilizer to a plant with wet, failing roots; it adds salt stress to an already damaged root system.'),
s('Light, temperature and change','A plant moved from a bright nursery greenhouse to a 1,000-lux living room will shed the leaves it can no longer support, usually the lower ones, over its first month. Fiddle-leaf fig and weeping fig (Ficus benjamina) are notorious for this. Fewer leaves in balance with the available light is the plant adapting, and moving it closer to the window is the fix.\n\nCold does similar damage faster. Tropicals exposed to temperatures below about 12–15 °C, whether by a winter draft, an open window or a cold car, often yellow and drop leaves a week or two later. Sudden bright sun causes a different yellow: bleached, pale patches on the leaves facing the glass, rather than whole leaves turning.'),
s('Nutrients, and what to do next','True nutrient deficiency is less common than people think in the first year after potting, because fresh mixes carry starter fertilizer. It becomes likely in plants that have been in the same mix for 2–3 years with little or no feeding. The fix is a complete houseplant fertilizer with magnesium and micronutrients at label rate during the growing season, not a single-nutrient supplement based on a guess.\n\nA yellow leaf will not turn green again. Leave a partly yellow leaf in place, since the plant is still recovering nutrients from it, and remove it once it is fully yellow or brown. Judge your correction by the new growth over the next 4–6 weeks, not by the old leaves.')
],['overwatering-vs-underwatering','root-rot-houseplants','fertilizing-houseplants'],[{title:'RHS: How to help a poorly houseplant',url:'https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/types/houseplants/how-to-help-a-poorly-houseplant'}]),
article('root-rot-houseplants','Root rot in houseplants: diagnosis, rescue and prevention','troubleshooting','Root rot starts with oxygen-starved roots in wet mix and ends with Pythium or Phytophthora. How to confirm it, cut it out, save cuttings and stop it coming back.','Root rot is an oxygen problem before it is a disease: cut away every soft root, repot into fresh mix in a pot sized to what survives, and water only after the mix dries to the right depth.',[
s('What is actually happening underground','Roots need oxygen to respire, just as leaves need light. When the spaces in the mix stay full of water, oxygen runs out within hours to days, and the fine root tips, the most active part of the root system, die first. That dead tissue is the entry point for water molds and fungi that live in almost every potting mix and garden: Pythium and Phytophthora (oomycetes, which spread through saturated mix by swimming spores) and Fusarium. Once established, they move from the dead tips into healthy roots and, eventually, into the stem base.\n\nThe triggers are almost always the same: watering too often for the light level, a pot much larger than the root ball, a dense or broken-down mix, a blocked drainage hole, or a cachepot holding a puddle. Cold makes it worse, because cool roots use less water and the mix stays wet longer.'),
s('Confirming it: above and below the soil','Above ground, the plant wilts or yellows even though the mix is wet, lower leaves drop, and growth stalls. On snake plant and ZZ the first sign is often a soft, translucent or darkened stem base, and the whole leaf may topple. Fungus gnats and a sour or swampy smell from the mix are frequent companions.\n\nThe only way to be sure is to look. Slide the plant out of the pot and inspect the roots:', ['Healthy roots are firm and springy, and white, cream, tan or orange depending on the species and mix.','Rotted roots are brown to black, soft or mushy, and often smell.','Pull gently on a suspect root: if the outer layer slides off and leaves a thin, stringy core, that root is dead.','Check the stem base and any rhizomes too: a firm rhizome can regrow roots, a soft one cannot.']),
s('Rescue surgery, step by step','Do not wait for spring if the plant is collapsing. Wash the old mix off the roots under lukewarm running water so you can see everything. With scissors wiped in 70% isopropyl alcohol, cut away every soft, brown root back to firm tissue, cleaning the blades between cuts. Discard the old mix; do not reuse it on another plant.\n\nRepot into fresh, open mix, such as a potting mix with a third of perlite or pumice added, in a pot only slightly larger than the remaining root system; after heavy pruning that is often a smaller pot than before. Scrub a reused pot with hot soapy water first. Water once lightly to settle the mix, place the plant in bright indirect light, and do not water again until the right drying depth for the species is reached. Hold off fertilizer until new growth appears, usually 4–8 weeks.'),
s('When the roots are gone: take cuttings','If the stem base is soft, or fewer than about a quarter of the roots are sound, save the plant through propagation while it still has healthy top growth. Pothos and heartleaf philodendron root easily from single-node cuttings in water or moist perlite, usually in 2–4 weeks at 20–25 °C. Monstera roots from a stem section with a node and an aerial root. Snake plant can be divided if some rhizomes are firm, or propagated from leaf sections, though leaf cuttings of the variegated ‘Laurentii’ lose their yellow edge. Individual ZZ leaflets will root, but slowly, over months.\n\nFungicide drenches cannot revive dead roots or overcome the conditions that caused the rot; if you use one, follow your local label.'),
s('Keeping it from coming back','Prevention is mostly about pot and water. Use pots with drainage holes and a size only 2–5 cm larger than the root ball, skip the gravel layer (it raises the saturated zone rather than removing it), and empty cachepots and saucers within 15–30 minutes of watering. Check the mix before every watering and stretch the intervals as light falls in autumn and winter. PlantPal reminders work well as a prompt to check, rather than an instruction to water.\n\nGive extra care to the plants most prone to rot. Snake plant, ZZ and aloe in a cool winter room may need water only every 3–6 weeks, and a single extra watering in December can be enough to start rot. Plants potted up too far at once, or sitting in a decorative pot without a drainage hole, are the other usual victims.')
],['overwatering-vs-underwatering','repotting-houseplants','potting-mix-vs-garden-soil']),
article('brown-leaf-tips','Brown leaf tips: causes, fixes and how to trim them','troubleshooting','Brown tips mark where water and salts run out at the leaf’s edge. Trace the cause — salts, fluoride, dry air, irregular watering or damaged roots — and fix it.','Brown tips are the end of the leaf’s water supply line: fix the water first (steady moisture, no softened water, flush salts with 2–3 pot volumes) before blaming humidity.',[
s('Why the tip goes first','Water enters through the roots and travels up through the leaf veins to the tip and margins, where much of it evaporates. That makes the tip the last stop on the line for two reasons. When water is short, whether from dry mix, damaged roots or very dry air pulling moisture out faster than roots can supply it, the tip is the first tissue to dry out and die. And whatever is dissolved in the water, fertilizer salts, sodium, fluoride or boron, is carried to the tip and left behind as the water evaporates, building up until the tissue there is poisoned.\n\nSo a brown tip is a symptom with several possible causes. The shape, color and timing of the damage narrow it down.'),
s('Reading the damage','Look at which leaves are affected and what the brown tissue looks like.', ['Crisp, tan tips on many leaves, with a thin yellow band between brown and green: salt or fluoride build-up, or chronic underwatering.','Brown tips mainly on older leaves, advancing slowly: accumulated salts; check for white crust on the mix and pot rim.','Sudden dark brown tips or edges after a feed: fertilizer burn from a strong dose on dry mix.','Brown edges plus curling on thin leaves in winter: dry indoor air, typically 20–35% RH with heating on.','Brown tips with yellowing lower leaves and wet mix: damaged roots; see the root-rot guide.','Brown patches in the middle of leaves facing the window: sunburn, not a tip problem.']),
s('Water quality and salts','Some plants are especially sensitive to fluoride and boron, which occur in certain municipal water supplies and in fertilizers: Dracaena species, spider plant (Chlorophytum comosum) and peace lily are the classic cases, and calatheas react badly too. If these plants brown at the tips despite good watering, switch to rainwater, distilled or reverse-osmosis water. Avoid water from a salt-based softener entirely; it replaces calcium with sodium, which accumulates in the mix and burns tips.\n\nFor salt build-up, stop feeding and flush the pot: slowly run clean water equal to 2–3 times the pot’s volume through the mix and let it drain completely. Do this only if the roots are healthy and the pot drains freely; flushing a waterlogged, rotting plant makes things worse. Then feed at half strength, always onto moist mix, and water to 10–20% runoff so salts keep leaving the pot.'),
s('Humidity and irregular watering','Heated homes in winter often sit at 20–35% relative humidity. Most common houseplants cope at 40–60%, but thin-leaved ferns, calatheas and some marantas prefer 50–70% and brown at the edges below it. Measure with a hygrometer at leaf height before assuming humidity is the problem. Misting raises humidity for only a few minutes and pebble trays have almost no effect on the room; a clean humidifier is the only reliable method, and grouping plants gives a small local boost.\n\nRepeated wilting is another common cause. A peace lily left to collapse between waterings browns at the tips and margins even though it perks up each time, so water it when only the top 1–2 cm is dry. Spider plants do the same when their fleshy roots are allowed to shrivel for weeks.'),
s('Trimming without making it worse','Brown tissue will not turn green again, so trimming is purely cosmetic. Use sharp, clean scissors and cut along the leaf’s natural outline, tapering to a point on pointed leaves. Leave a hair-thin line of brown at the edge: cutting into green tissue creates a fresh wound that often browns again, restarting the problem.\n\nAfter you correct the cause, the test is new growth. If the next two or three leaves open clean, the problem is solved, even if older leaves keep their scars.')
],['how-to-water-houseplants','fertilizer-burn-houseplants','houseplant-humidity']),
article('fungus-gnats-houseplants','Fungus gnats: how to break the four-week breeding cycle in potting mix','troubleshooting','Fungus gnat larvae live in the top 5 cm of damp potting mix. Learn their life cycle, how to find the source pot, and how to combine drying, BTi and sticky cards.','Treat the soil, not the air: keep the top 2–5 cm drier where the plant allows and treat the larvae for at least 3–4 weeks, one full gnat generation.',[
s('Know the life cycle you are interrupting','Fungus gnats (Bradysia spp.) are weak-flying black flies about 2–3 mm long that run across the soil surface and lift off in a small cloud when you water. The adults do little direct harm and live only about a week, but in that week the females lay their eggs by the dozen in the top few centimetres of moist, organic potting mix.\n\nThe larvae are the stage that matters. Translucent, 4–6 mm long and black-headed, they live in the top ~5 cm of mix, eating fungi and decaying matter and, in large numbers, fine root hairs and the bases of cuttings. Egg to adult takes roughly 3–4 weeks at room temperature, which is why spraying the flies never ends an infestation: new adults keep emerging from the soil for weeks.'),
s('Confirm the pest and find the source pot','Check that you are dealing with gnats at all. Fruit flies (Drosophila) are tan with red eyes and gather around fruit bowls and bins; drain flies are fuzzy, with moth-like wings, and rest on bathroom walls near sinks.\n\nTo find the pots that are actually breeding, press a 1 cm slice of raw potato onto the mix surface and lift it after 2–3 days; larvae gather on its underside. Stick a yellow sticky card just above the soil in each suspect pot and count the catch weekly. In most homes one or two chronically wet pots produce most of the flies, often a plant in a large pot on a dim shelf. Keep the cards out of reach of pets and children.'),
s('Take away the damp top layer','Larvae need the top layer to stay moist to survive. For plants that tolerate it (pothos, monstera, rubber plant, philodendron), let the top 2–5 cm dry fully before watering again; snake plant, ZZ and aloe can dry much deeper. Empty cachepots and saucers within 15–30 minutes of watering, and pick fallen leaves off the surface. Peace lilies and ferns cannot go that dry without damage, so rely on the biological controls below for them.\n\nA 1–2 cm top dressing of coarse sand or fine grit dries within hours and discourages egg-laying. Keep opened bags of potting mix sealed and dry: a bag stored damp in a shed is a common source of a fresh infestation. Skip cinnamon and peroxide drenches: the evidence is thin, and strong peroxide can damage root tips.'),
s('Use biological controls that reach the larvae','BTi (Bacillus thuringiensis subsp. israelensis) produces a protein that is toxic only to the larvae of flies such as mosquitoes and gnats. It is sold as granules or floating “dunks” that you soak in water and use to drench the mix. It only works when larvae eat it and does not kill eggs or adults, so apply it every 5–7 days for 3–4 applications to cover a whole generation. Follow your local product label for rates.\n\nFor larger collections, the beneficial nematode Steinernema feltiae can be watered into moist mix, where it hunts larvae for several weeks. Use it soon after purchase, since the nematodes are alive and perishable. Stratiolaelaps scimitus (formerly Hypoaspis) predatory mites live in the top layer of the mix and eat gnat larvae and thrips pupae, which makes them a good standing defence in a greenhouse or plant room.', ['Week 0: put one yellow card in each affected pot and note the counts.','Weeks 0–3: drench with BTi every 5–7 days (3–4 applications), or apply nematodes once.','Throughout: let the top 2–5 cm dry where the species allows, and empty saucers.','Week 4: compare card counts. A steady fall means the cycle is broken.']),
s('Judge success by the trend','Expect to keep catching adults for 1–2 weeks after you start, because larvae that were already pupating will still emerge. What matters is the direction of the weekly count per card: a pot that goes from 30 gnats to 5 to 0 over a month is clean, even if you saw a fly this morning.\n\nIf counts plateau, look for a missed source such as a forgotten propagation jar with a soggy plug, a self-watering planter or a new plant. Keep new arrivals in quarantine with a sticky card for their first few weeks so they never reach the rest of your collection.')
],['how-to-water-houseplants','quarantine-new-houseplants','potting-mix-vs-garden-soil']),
article('spider-mites-houseplants','Spider mites on houseplants: catch them before the webbing','troubleshooting','Two-spotted spider mites complete a generation in 7–10 days in warm, dry rooms. Learn the early stippling signs, how to confirm them, and a treatment schedule that works.','By the time you see webbing, the colony is several generations old. Check leaf undersides weekly with a 10× lens and treat every 5–7 days for at least three rounds.',[
s('Why mites explode indoors','The two-spotted spider mite (Tetranychus urticae) is an arachnid, not an insect. Adults are 0.4–0.5 mm long, barely visible as moving specks, with two dark patches on the body. At 25–30 °C a mite goes from egg to egg-laying adult in about 7–10 days, and generations overlap, so a few mites on a new plant can become thousands within a month.\n\nWarm, dry air suits them. Heated rooms in winter often sit at 20–35% relative humidity, which is ideal for mites and stressful for the plant. Frequent hosts include fiddle-leaf fig (Ficus lyrata), Alocasia, calatheas, English ivy (Hedera helix), Schefflera, croton (Codiaeum) and indoor palms. Because mites are arachnids, many general-purpose insecticides do little against them.'),
s('Read the early signs','Mites feed by piercing single leaf cells and emptying them, and each emptied cell shows as a pinprick of pale yellow or white on the upper leaf surface. This stippling is the first sign. As damage builds, leaves take on a dull, greyish or bronzed cast, then yellow and drop. Fine silk webbing between the leaf stalk and stem, or across new growth, only appears once numbers are high.', ['Turn leaves over and scan along the midrib and veins with a 10× hand lens.','Tap a branch over white paper; mites show as moving dots that smear green or reddish.','Check the newest leaves and growing tips, where colonies often start.','Inspect every plant whose foliage touches an affected one.','Rule out dust, old sunburn and thrips, whose damage is silvery streaks with black specks.']),
s('Knock the numbers down physically','Move the plant away from the rest of the collection at once; mites spread by crawling across touching leaves and drift on air currents. Cover the pot surface with a plastic bag and shower the foliage with lukewarm water, directing the spray at the undersides. On large-leaved plants, wipe both sides of each leaf with a damp soft cloth.\n\nCut off leaves that are mostly bronzed and bag them before they leave the room. Wipe down the shelf, windowsill and pot. A thorough rinse removes a large share of the active mites, but the eggs stay glued to the leaf, so it is only the first step.'),
s('Treat on the mite’s calendar','Insecticidal soap (potassium salts of fatty acids) and horticultural oil kill by contact and leave no residual activity. Coverage of the leaf undersides is everything. Because eggs survive, repeat the treatment every 5–7 days for at least three applications so each new hatch is caught before it can lay. Test on one leaf and wait 24–48 hours first, avoid spraying in direct sun or above about 30 °C, and follow your local product label. Household dish detergent is not a formulated insecticidal soap and can scorch foliage.\n\nFor persistent or large infestations, the predatory mite Phytoseiulus persimilis eats only spider mites and can clear a collection biologically; it works best in moderate warmth and humidity and should not be sprayed with soap or oil after release. Raising humidity to 50–60% slows mite reproduction but will not eradicate a colony on its own. If you use a labelled miticide, rotate active ingredients, because mites develop resistance quickly.'),
s('Decide when it is really over','Old stippling never greens up again, so judge progress by new leaves and live mites. Declare the plant clean only after two consecutive weekly checks with a lens show no moving mites or fresh stippling, and keep checking its neighbours for another 3–4 weeks.\n\nAfterwards, make a quick underside check part of your weekly watering round for mite-prone species, especially from late autumn, when the heating comes on and the air dries out.')
],['quarantine-new-houseplants','brown-leaf-tips','houseplant-humidity']),
article('fertilizer-burn-houseplants','Fertilizer burn in houseplants: diagnose it, flush it, recover','troubleshooting','Brown leaf margins, dead root tips and a white crust on the mix point to soluble-salt injury. Learn how fertilizer burn happens, how to leach a pot and when to repot.','If the roots and drainage are sound, flush the pot with 2–3 times its volume of clean water, then feed nothing until healthy new leaves unfold.',[
s('What “burn” really is','Fertilizers are salts. Dissolved at the right strength (an electrical conductivity of roughly 0.8–1.5 mS/cm for most foliage houseplants), they feed the plant. Too concentrated, they raise the salt level of the soil solution until water moves out of the root cells instead of in, which is osmotic stress. The finest root tips die first, and the plant shows drought symptoms while sitting in wet mix.\n\nOn the leaves, damage appears where transpired water ends its journey: the tips and margins. Salts concentrate there as water evaporates, and the tissue browns from the edge inward, often with a yellow band between the dead and healthy tissue. A single double-strength feed rarely does this to a healthy plant. Repeated overdoses, or salts building up over months, do.'),
s('Symptoms and look-alikes','Typical signs are sudden wilting or leaf drop in moist mix within a few days of feeding; crisp brown tips and margins on several leaves at once; a white or yellowish crust on the mix surface or the rim of a terracotta pot; and roots with brown, dead tips that slide off when pulled gently. Growth stalls because a damaged root system cannot take up water or nutrients.\n\nSeveral problems look similar. Dracaena, spider plant (Chlorophytum comosum) and peace lily are sensitive to fluoride and boron in tap water and get tip burn even without overfeeding; underwatering and cold draughts also brown leaf edges. The clues that point to fertilizer are timing (damage soon after feeding), a salt crust, and many leaves affected at once rather than one or two old ones.'),
s('How pots end up oversalted','Most cases trace back to routine habits rather than one big mistake. Check your own routine against this list before you change anything else.', ['Measuring a concentrate with the wrong spoon, or mixing a dose meant for 4 L into a 1 L can.','Feeding a bone-dry root ball, which puts concentrated solution straight onto stressed root tips. Water first, feed next time.','Stacking sources: a mix that “feeds for up to 6 months”, plus controlled-release granules, plus liquid feed.','Feeding at full rate through winter, when low light slows uptake and salts accumulate.','Only ever giving small sips that never drain, so salts are never flushed out.','Using softened water, which swaps calcium for sodium.']),
s('Leach the pot or repot it','If drainage works and most roots are still firm and pale, leach. Take the plant to a sink or bath and slowly pour room-temperature water through the mix, 2–3 times the pot’s volume (2–3 L for a 1 L pot), letting it run freely out of the holes. Scrape off any visible surface granules and the top 1 cm of crusted mix first. Let it drain completely, then return it to bright indirect light and do not water again until the top 2–5 cm is dry.\n\nIf the roots are mostly brown and mushy, or the mix is compacted and sour-smelling, leaching will only drown what is left. Instead, repot into fresh, unfertilized mix in a pot no larger than the healthy root mass, trimming dead roots with clean scissors. Do not add vinegar, baking soda or other home “neutralizers”; they add more salts or shift pH without removing anything.'),
s('Restart feeding carefully','Brown margins will not recover. Trim them if you like, following the leaf’s natural outline and leaving a thin line of brown so you do not cut into live tissue. Wait for 2–3 healthy new leaves, typically 4–6 weeks in the growing season, before feeding again.\n\nThen resume at half the label rate every 2–4 weeks from spring to late summer, and pause in winter unless the plant is under grow lights. Water so that 10–20% runs out of the bottom each time. That steady runoff is the best way to stop salts from building up again.')
],['fertilizing-houseplants','npk-fertilizer-numbers','root-rot-houseplants']),
article('bottom-watering-houseplants','Bottom watering houseplants: how long to soak and when to top water instead','plant-care','Bottom watering rewets a dry root ball evenly from below. Learn soak times, which plants benefit most, why salts build up, and when top watering works better.','Soak for 10–30 minutes until the surface darkens, drain fully, and top water to runoff every fourth or fifth time to flush out salts.',[
s('How water climbs from the basin','Bottom watering relies on capillary rise: water is drawn up through the small pores between particles of the mix, the way a paper towel wicks a spill. Fine peat- and coir-based mixes wick well. Chunky aroid blends with lots of bark and coarse perlite have large pores that break the capillary chain, so the top of the pot can stay dry however long it sits.\n\nPot height matters too. In a pot of standard mix deeper than about 20 cm, the top layer may never wet from below. That is the main reason bottom watering works best for small and medium pots of fine-textured mix, and why you should always check that the surface has darkened rather than assuming it has.'),
s('A soak routine that works','Soaking is a controlled, supervised process, not a way of leaving plants in water.', ['Use a basin or sink with 2–5 cm of room-temperature water; the pot must have open drainage holes.','Stand the pot in it for 10–30 minutes, until the surface darkens or the pot feels clearly heavier.','Top up the basin if the plant drinks it all within the first few minutes; very dry mix is thirsty.','Lift the pot out and let it drain on a rack for 10–15 minutes before it goes back in its cachepot.','Never leave a pot sitting in water for hours; saturated mix runs short of oxygen and roots start to suffocate.']),
s('Plants that gain the most','Bottom watering is most valuable for plants whose leaves or crowns dislike being wet. African violets (Saintpaulia) and Cape primroses (Streptocarpus) develop pale rings when cold water splashes their leaves. Cyclamen rot readily if water collects on the tuber. Snake plant (Dracaena trifasciata) rots at the base when water pools in the leaf rosette.\n\nIt is also the best rescue for a root ball that has dried so hard it has shrunk from the pot wall. Dry peat and coir become water-repellent, and top water simply runs down the gap and out the bottom. A 20–30 minute soak rewets the whole mass. Seed trays and freshly sown pots benefit as well, because top watering washes seeds out of place.'),
s('Where bottom watering goes wrong','Water enters from below and evaporates from the top, carrying dissolved minerals and fertilizer salts up with it. Over months these concentrate in the upper layer and show as a white crust. The fix is simple: every fourth or fifth watering, water from the top until 10–20% runs out of the drainage holes, flushing the salts down and out. For the same reason, it is better to apply liquid fertilizer from the top than to mix it into the soak water.\n\nA shared basin is also a shared disease route. Water moulds such as Pythium spread through standing water, and fungus gnat larvae can move between pots. Soak healthy plants together if you must, but put any plant with yellowing, wilting or suspected root rot in its own container, and refresh the water between batches.')
],['how-to-water-houseplants','overwatering-vs-underwatering','fertilizer-burn-houseplants']),
article('using-a-plant-light-meter','How to use a plant light meter: lux readings that mean something','plant-care','Lux readings help only if you take them the same way every time. Learn where to hold the sensor, what the numbers mean for plants, and the limits of phone meters.','Measure at leaf height with the sensor facing the window, around midday, on several days. Below about 2,500 lux most foliage plants just hold on; 10,000–20,000 lux is bright indirect light.',[
s('Take a repeatable reading','Put the sensor where the leaves are, at the top of the plant’s canopy, and point it towards the main light source, usually the window, not at the ceiling. On a phone the ambient light sensor usually sits beside the front camera near the earpiece, so hold the screen facing the window. Keep your hand, head and shadow out of the way.\n\nTake three readings a few seconds apart and use the middle value. Note the time, date and sky conditions. Readings are only comparable when they are taken the same way, and a spot measured at noon on a sunny day cannot be compared fairly with one measured at 5 pm under cloud.'),
s('What the numbers mean','These bands are approximate midday values at leaf height. Use them to place a plant, not to prescribe its care to the last lux.', ['Typical room away from windows: 50–500 lux. Too dark for any plant long term.','Low light: 500–2,500 lux. Survival for ZZ, snake plant, cast iron plant, green pothos.','Medium light: 2,500–10,000 lux. Steady growth for most foliage plants.','Bright indirect: 10,000–20,000 lux. Ideal for monstera, fiddle-leaf fig and variegated plants.','Direct sun through glass: 20,000–50,000+ lux. Suits aloe, cacti and succulents.','Full outdoor sun: around 100,000 lux.']),
s('From an instant reading to a daily light budget','Plants respond to the total light they receive in a day, the daily light integral (DLI, in mol/m²/day), not to a single instant. A south-facing sill (in the Northern Hemisphere) might read 30,000 lux at noon but only for a few hours; a north window might read 3,000 lux almost all day. Measure several times a day to see the shape of the curve.\n\nLux is weighted to human vision, while photosynthesis is measured as PPFD (µmol/m²/s). For sunlight, roughly 54 lux equals 1 µmol/m²/s, so 10,000 lux of daylight is about 185 µmol/m²/s. Held for 8 hours, that gives about 5 mol/m²/day (PPFD × hours × 0.0036), a comfortable level for most foliage plants. Grow lights have different spectra, and a pink or purple LED can read deceptively low in lux, so for lamps rely on the manufacturer’s PPFD figures at a given distance.'),
s('Know your meter’s limits','Phone sensors are not calibrated horticultural instruments. They differ between models, can saturate in direct sun, and read differently depending on the angle at which light strikes them. They are very good at comparisons, telling you that one shelf is three times brighter than another, and less trustworthy for absolute values. PlantPal’s light meter is built for exactly that kind of comparison: same technique, same spot, repeated over time.\n\nIf you want more precision, a dedicated lux meter is inexpensive, and a quantum (PAR) meter measures PPFD directly, which is what serious growers use under grow lights.'),
s('Turn readings into decisions','Light falls off fast with distance from a window: a plant 2 m back often gets only 10–20% of what reaches the glass. If a plant is stretching, producing small leaves or losing variegation and its spot reads under 2,500 lux, move it closer to the light. Do this in steps over 1–2 weeks if it will now get direct sun, and watch for bleached or scorched patches.\n\nMeasure again at the change of seasons. The winter sun is lower and reaches deeper into a room, but days are short and intensity drops, so a spot that was ideal in June can be too dim in December.')
],['bright-indirect-light','low-light-houseplants','houseplant-care-for-beginners']),
article('houseplant-humidity','Houseplant humidity: what actually raises it, and what doesn’t','plant-care','Heated homes often drop to 20–35% humidity in winter. Learn which houseplants really need more, why misting and pebble trays barely help, and how to run a humidifier.','Most common houseplants cope at 40–60% relative humidity. Only thin-leaved plants such as calatheas and ferns need 50–70%, and a clean humidifier is the only reliable way to get there.',[
s('Why winter air gets so dry','Relative humidity (RH) is how much water vapour the air holds compared with the maximum it could hold at that temperature, and warm air can hold far more. Outdoor air at 0 °C and 80% RH, warmed to 21 °C inside your home, drops to about 20% RH without losing a drop of water. That is why heated rooms in winter commonly sit at 20–35% RH, drier than many deserts.\n\nDry air pulls water out of leaves faster through their stomata. Plants with thick, waxy cuticles barely notice, while thin-leaved species lose water faster than their roots can replace it, and their leaf edges and tips dry out and brown.'),
s('Measure before you buy anything','A digital hygrometer costs little and ends the guesswork. Place it at leaf height among the plants, away from windows, radiators and any humidifier plume, and check it in the morning and evening for a few days. Most consumer units are accurate to within about ±3–5% RH, which is plenty.\n\nDo not treat brown tips as proof that the air is dry. Fluoride in tap water, salt build-up, irregular watering and root damage cause the same symptom. If the hygrometer reads 45% and the tips are still browning, look at the roots and your water before you look at the air.'),
s('Which plants actually care','Leaf structure predicts humidity needs better than any label. Thick, leathery or succulent leaves hold water well; thin, papery leaves do not.', ['Snake plant, ZZ, aloe, rubber plant (Ficus elastica), Hoya: 30–50% RH is fine.','Monstera, pothos, philodendron, peace lily, spider plant: cope well at 40–60%.','Calatheas (Goeppertia), prayer plant (Maranta), nerve plant (Fittonia), maidenhair fern (Adiantum): prefer 50–70%.','Signs of air that is too dry for the plant: crisp margins on new leaves, and new leaves that stick or tear as they unfurl.']),
s('Misting, pebble trays and grouping: the honest numbers','Misting raises the humidity around a plant for a few minutes; in a ventilated room the droplets evaporate and disperse almost immediately. Frequent misting in still air can do more harm than good, because leaves that stay wet invite fungal and bacterial leaf spots. Pebble trays have a negligible effect at room scale; their main use is keeping a pot above its drainage water.\n\nGrouping plants gives a small local benefit, since each one transpires into the shared air. For a handful of genuinely humidity-hungry plants, an enclosed glass cabinet or large terrarium works well, provided it has a small fan so the air does not stagnate. A bathroom helps only if it also has enough light.'),
s('Run a humidifier properly','A humidifier is the only practical way to hold a room at 50% or more. Evaporative models push air through a wet wick and cannot over-humidify easily. Ultrasonic models are quiet, but with hard tap water they throw out fine white mineral dust, so fill them with distilled or demineralised water. Size the unit to the room using the manufacturer’s figures, and empty, rinse and clean the tank weekly to stop bacterial and mould growth.\n\nAim for 45–55% in winter. Above about 60%, water condenses on cold window glass and walls, which leads to mould on frames and plaster. A side benefit: raising the humidity also slows spider mites, which thrive in exactly the warm, dry air that heated rooms produce.')
],['brown-leaf-tips','spider-mites-houseplants','organize-plants-by-zone']),
article('low-light-houseplants','Low-light houseplants: what “tolerant” really means, in lux','plant-guides','Low light means roughly 500–2,500 lux, not a windowless hallway. See which houseplants truly tolerate it, how their care changes, and when to add a grow light.','Low-light plants survive at 500–2,500 lux but grow slowly. Below about 500 lux even the toughest need a grow light, and all of them need far less water than in a bright spot.',[
s('Put a number on “low light”','Your eyes are a poor judge of plant light because they adapt constantly. A living room that feels pleasantly bright often measures only 50–500 lux a couple of metres from the windows, while plants call 500–2,500 lux “low light”. That band is roughly what you find 1–2 m from a north-facing window (in the Northern Hemisphere), or at the back of a room with a large east or west window.\n\nBelow about 500 lux, a plant cannot make as much sugar as it burns, and it lives off its reserves. A ZZ or snake plant can look unchanged for months in a dark corner while slowly running down, which is why “it’s been fine for a year” is not proof that a spot is bright enough.'),
s('The genuinely tolerant shortlist','These plants accept low light and still keep healthy foliage. None of them grows fast there, and most would grow better with more light.', ['ZZ plant (Zamioculcas zamiifolia): potato-like rhizomes store water and carbohydrates.','Snake plant (Dracaena trifasciata): CAM photosynthesis and thick leaves make it very frugal.','Cast iron plant (Aspidistra elatior): the classic dark-hallway plant, and non-toxic to pets per the ASPCA.','Green pothos (Epipremnum aureum ‘Jade’) and heartleaf philodendron (Philodendron hederaceum): trail happily at 1,000–2,500 lux.','Chinese evergreen (Aglaonema), green cultivars: soft, patterned leaves that suit shade.','Peace lily (Spathiphyllum): survives in low light but rarely flowers there.']),
s('Why these plants cope, and what they give up','Shade-tolerant species build “shade leaves”: thinner, broader, with more chlorophyll per unit area, so they break even at a lower light level than sun plants. Many also store resources, in rhizomes (ZZ, snake plant) or thick stems, which lets them ride out long dim spells.\n\nThe cost is growth. Expect a few new leaves a year, not a flush every month. Variegated cultivars such as pothos ‘Marble Queen’ or snake plant ‘Laurentii’ have less chlorophyll in their pale tissue, so they struggle more in the same spot and often lose contrast or revert to green. Keep the variegated plants for your brighter spots. Note that ZZ, snake plant, pothos, philodendron, Aglaonema and peace lily are all listed by the ASPCA as toxic to cats and dogs.'),
s('Water and feed for a slower plant','Less light means less photosynthesis, less transpiration and a pot that dries two to three times more slowly than the same pot near a bright window. This is where most low-light plants die: they are kept on a “bright room” watering schedule and rot. Water pothos and philodendron only when the top 2–5 cm is dry, which may take 2–3 weeks, and leave snake plant and ZZ until the mix is dry through most of the pot, often every 4–6 weeks in winter.\n\nKeep pots snug, only 2–5 cm wider than the root ball; a big pot of wet mix in a dim corner is a root-rot setup. Feed lightly, at half rate a few times during spring and summer, because a slow plant cannot use much, and the unused salts accumulate.'),
s('Signs it’s too dark, and the grow-light fix','Watch for long gaps between leaves, new leaves noticeably smaller than old ones, stems leaning hard towards the window, fading variegation, older leaves yellowing faster than new ones appear, or no new growth at all through an entire spring and summer. Any of these means the plant is running a deficit.\n\nEither move it closer to the window in steps over a week or two, or add light. A plain full-spectrum white LED on a timer for 12–14 hours a day, placed at the distance the manufacturer gives for foliage plants, can turn a 300-lux shelf into a 5,000-lux one and let you grow almost anything there.')
],['zz-plant-care','snake-plant-care','pothos-care','bright-indirect-light']),
article('vacation-plant-care','Vacation plant care: how to prepare for one, two or four weeks away','tips','How to prepare houseplants for a week, a fortnight or a month away: pre-trip watering, moving plants out of hot windows, tested wicks, and clear sitter instructions.','Water each plant by its own rule 1–2 days before you leave and pull sun-lovers back from hot glass. For trips over about two weeks, arrange a sitter rather than trusting a reservoir.',[
s('Match the plan to the trip','Most healthy houseplants in suitable pots handle a week alone without help. The risk grows with trip length, summer heat and small pots, which dry fastest. Plan for your longest-to-dry plants and your thirstiest ones separately.', ['Up to 7 days: water normally 1–2 days before leaving; nothing else needed for most plants.','8–14 days: move thirsty plants out of direct sun, group them, and use a tested wick or one sitter visit halfway through.','15 days or more: a sitter every 7–10 days, with written notes for each plant.','Snake plant, ZZ, aloe and other succulents: water only if they are already dry. They handle 3–4 weeks without water easily.']),
s('Slow down water use before you go','A plant’s water use is driven by light and heat. Pulling plants 1–2 m back from south- or west-facing windows (north- or west-facing in the Southern Hemisphere) cuts their light and transpiration without harming them for a few weeks. In summer, closing blinds on sunny windows keeps the room cooler; in winter, never let the heating drop below 12–15 °C, the minimum for monstera, pothos, peace lily and fiddle-leaf fig.\n\nWater thoroughly until 10–20% runs out, then empty every saucer and cachepot. Do not leave plants standing in water “to be safe”: after a week in a flooded saucer the lower mix is airless and the roots start to die. Skip fertilizing, repotting and pruning in the two weeks before departure so you do not leave a stressed plant with no one to watch it.'),
s('DIY watering aids that actually work','A capillary wick is the most reliable low-tech option: a cotton or nylon cord running from a jug of water into the mix, pushed about 5 cm deep near the roots, with the jug at the same height as the pot or slightly above. For a group of plants, stand the pots on a thick, wet towel in a bath or sink with 1–2 cm of water, provided the room has a window. Small humidity-lovers such as ferns can go inside a loose clear plastic tent, kept out of direct sun so they do not cook.\n\nWhatever you choose, run it for a full week while you are still at home. Wicks can deliver far more or far less than you expect depending on the cord and the mix, and you want to find that out before the trip, not after it.'),
s('Brief your sitter plant by plant','“Water everything on Saturday” is how sitters kill cacti and let peace lilies collapse. Give instructions they can check for themselves: “push a finger in to the second knuckle; if it’s dry, water until it drips from the bottom, then empty the saucer within half an hour.” Put a coloured tag in each pot, say green for check-and-water and red for do not water, and leave a photo of each plant with its name.\n\nPlantPal’s Plant Sitter feature gathers clear care instructions for each plant in one place for the person looking after them, which beats a long note stuck to the fridge. Leave your contact details, or those of a plant-savvy friend, for anything that looks wrong.'),
s('Coming home','Resist the urge to water everything the moment you walk in. Check each pot first: the sitter may have watered yesterday. A wilted peace lily usually recovers within hours of a thorough watering; a root ball that has dried rock-hard and shrunk from the pot wall is best rewetted by standing it in water for 20–30 minutes. Move plants back to their usual positions and, if you used a reservoir or wick, flush them with a top watering to clear any accumulated salts.')
],['how-to-water-houseplants','organize-plants-by-zone','self-watering-pots']),
article('quarantine-new-houseplants','Quarantine new houseplants: a four-week inspection routine','tips','New plants are the main way pests reach a collection. How to inspect before you buy, set up a 2–4 week quarantine, and what to check weekly with a 10× hand lens.','Keep every new plant 2–4 weeks away from your collection and check it weekly with a 10× lens. That is long enough for hidden eggs to hatch into pests you can see.',[
s('Why two to four weeks','At the moment of purchase most pest problems are invisible: spider mite eggs are 0.1 mm spheres on the leaf underside, mealybug eggs sit inside cottony sacs in leaf axils, thrips eggs are inserted into leaf tissue, and fungus gnat larvae live under the soil surface. Their cycles set the length of quarantine. Spider mites go from egg to adult in 7–10 days in warm rooms, thrips in roughly 2–3 weeks, and fungus gnats in 3–4 weeks. Two to four weeks lets at least one generation reveal itself.\n\nCommercial growers also treat plants on a preventive schedule, and those treatments can hold pests below visible levels for a while after the plant leaves the nursery. A clean-looking plant on day one is not yet a clean plant.'),
s('Inspect before you pay','Two minutes at the shop saves weeks of treatment later. Take a small hand lens or use your phone camera zoomed in, and look where pests hide rather than at the side of the plant displayed to customers.', ['Leaf undersides along the midrib: pale stippling, moving specks or fine webbing (spider mites).','Leaf axils, stem joints and under leaf sheaths: white cottony tufts (mealybugs) or brown bumps that scrape off (scale).','Silvery streaks with tiny black dots of excrement on leaves: thrips.','Sticky honeydew, or black sooty mould on the leaves below it.','Tap the pot: small black flies lifting off the surface are fungus gnats.','If the shop allows, slide the root ball out: roots should be firm and pale, not brown, mushy or sour-smelling.']),
s('Set up the quarantine spot','Use a separate room if you can; if not, keep at least a couple of metres between the newcomer and your other plants, as spider mites drift on air currents and walk across touching leaves. The spot still needs appropriate light and temperature, or you will confuse stress symptoms with disease. Put a yellow sticky card in the pot to monitor gnats and thrips.\n\nOn arrival, rinse the foliage under a lukewarm shower (with the pot surface covered) and wipe large leaves on both sides. This removes a good share of any mobile pests and dust. Water and handle the quarantined plant last in your routine, and wash your hands and tools afterwards.'),
s('Weekly checks, and what to do if you find something','Once a week, go over the plant with a 10× lens, focusing on new growth, leaf undersides and leaf axils, and count the sticky card. If you find something, identify it before you reach for a spray, then follow the specific treatment for that pest. A few mealybugs can be dabbed off with a cotton swab dipped in 70% isopropyl alcohol (test on one leaf first); spider mites, thrips and gnats need repeated treatments timed to their life cycles.\n\nRestart the quarantine clock from the last treatment: the plant joins the collection only after two or three clean weekly checks in a row. A plant that is heavily infested with mealybugs or thrips is often better returned or bagged and discarded than nursed alongside a healthy collection.'),
s('Let it settle before changing its care','A move from a nursery greenhouse to a living room is a big drop in light and humidity, and some leaf drop is normal, especially in fiddle-leaf fig (Ficus lyrata) and weeping fig (Ficus benjamina), which shed leaves after almost any change. Do not repot in the first 3–4 weeks unless the plant is waterlogged or badly root-bound; use that time to learn how quickly its existing pot dries. Nursery mixes are often dense and peat-heavy and dry more slowly than you would expect.\n\nHold off on fertilizer too. Most nursery plants arrive with enough controlled-release feed in the mix to last several weeks.')
],['spider-mites-houseplants','fungus-gnats-houseplants','houseplant-care-for-beginners']),
article('organize-plants-by-zone','Organize houseplants by zone: group by light, not just by room','tips','Grouping houseplants into care zones by light, temperature and drying speed turns plant care into a quick weekly circuit. Here’s how to map your home into zones.','Define each zone by its measured light and how fast pots dry there, then check one zone at a time. A single room can easily hold two or three very different zones.',[
s('What makes a zone a zone','Greenhouse growers do not organize benches by which door is nearest; they group crops that share light, temperature and irrigation needs. Do the same at home. A zone is a spot where growing conditions are roughly uniform. In one living room, a south-facing sill reading 30,000 lux at noon and a bookcase on the back wall reading 300 lux are two different zones, even though they are 4 m apart.\n\nName zones by place and condition, such as “Kitchen – east sill”, “Living room – bright corner” or “Hall – low light”, so the name itself reminds you, or a sitter, what the plants there can expect.'),
s('Map your home in an afternoon','Walk the house once with a light meter and a notepad, and write down for each potential spot the few facts that actually drive care.', ['Midday light at leaf height, in lux, and the window direction.','Hours of direct sun on the leaves, and whether it is morning or afternoon sun.','Heat sources and draughts: radiators, air-conditioning vents, ovens, often-opened doors.','Winter cold: a sill behind a closed curtain can drop below 10 °C at night.','Humidity: kitchens and bathrooms run higher than a heated living room.']),
s('Match plants to zones','With the map in hand, put plants where their needs match the conditions. Bright zones (10,000 lux and up, some direct sun) suit aloe, cacti, variegated rubber plants such as Ficus elastica ‘Tineke’, and fiddle-leaf fig. Medium zones (2,500–10,000 lux) suit monstera, most philodendrons, pothos ‘Marble Queen’ and a flowering peace lily. Low zones (500–2,500 lux) are for ZZ, snake plant and cast iron plant.\n\nWithin a zone, try to group plants that dry at a similar pace, so that one check covers several pots. Keep humidity-lovers such as calatheas and ferns clustered together, ideally near a humidifier; grouping gives them a small shared microclimate. Keep plants that are toxic to pets, such as monstera, pothos and philodendron, in zones your cat cannot reach.'),
s('Walk a weekly circuit','Take the watering can and do one zone at a time. In each zone, check the fastest-drying pots first: small pots, terracotta and anything in direct sun. Then check the slow ones, and leave anything still moist alone. As you go, glance at leaf undersides for pests and empty any saucers. A 20-plant collection can be covered properly in 10–15 minutes this way.\n\nZones change with the seasons. In winter, south-facing zones become more valuable and north-facing ones may drop below useful light, while pots in radiator zones dry out much faster. When a plant has to change zones, move it in steps over 1–2 weeks, and expect its watering interval to change.'),
s('Keep the zone map current','PlantPal lets you sort your plants into custom zones that match your home, which makes your weekly circuit and any sitter handover follow the same map. Update a plant’s zone the day you move it, because its old care rhythm no longer applies.\n\nRe-measure and review the zones twice a year, around the spring and autumn equinoxes, when sun angles shift most. Trees that leaf out, new curtains and plants growing into each other’s light all change a zone more than you notice day to day.')
],['vacation-plant-care','using-a-plant-light-meter','houseplant-care-for-beginners']),
article('terracotta-vs-plastic-pots','Terracotta vs. plastic pots: how pot material changes watering','soil-fertilizer','Unglazed terracotta loses water through its walls and dries much faster than plastic. Learn which plants suit each, why salts crust on clay, and how to adjust watering.','Use terracotta for plants that like to dry out, such as succulents, snake plant and ZZ, and plastic or glazed pots for plants that want even moisture, such as peace lily and ferns.',[
s('What the pot wall does','Unglazed terracotta is fired clay full of microscopic pores. Water wicks from the mix into the wall and evaporates from the outside surface, so the pot loses moisture from its whole side area, not just the top. That evaporation also cools the root zone slightly and lets a little air in at the edge of the root ball, which is why roots often cling to the inside of a clay pot. In a warm, bright room a terracotta pot typically dries markedly faster than an identical plastic one.\n\nPlastic is impermeable, so water leaves only through the surface and the drainage holes, and the mix stays evenly moist for longer. Glazed ceramic, painted or sealed terracotta, and resin or fibreglass planters all behave like plastic, whatever they look like.'),
s('Where terracotta earns its place','Clay suits plants that want their mix to dry deeply between waterings: cacti and succulents, aloe, snake plant (Dracaena trifasciata), ZZ and Hoya. It is also forgiving for anyone who tends to overwater, since the pot works against a soggy root zone. Its weight steadies tall or top-heavy plants that would tip a plastic pot.\n\nThe downsides are real. Clay breaks, and a 30 cm pot of wet mix is heavy to move. Minerals from hard water and fertilizer salts wick out through the wall and dry as a white crust on the outside and rim; leaves or stems resting on a crusted rim can scorch. Green algae on the outside is harmless. Soak a new, bone-dry terracotta pot in water for about 30 minutes before planting, or it will pull water out of the fresh mix and the new plant.'),
s('Where plastic wins','Plastic is the better choice for plants that dislike drying out, such as peace lily (Spathiphyllum), Boston fern (Nephrolepis exaltata), calatheas and maidenhair fern, and for any plant in a hot, sunny window where clay would need water every day or two. It is also practical for hanging baskets and large specimens, where weight matters, and as a lightweight inner pot inside a decorative cachepot.\n\nThe risk runs the other way. In low light, or in a pot much bigger than the root ball, plastic keeps mix wet long enough for roots to suffocate and rot. If you choose plastic, choose a snug size, 2–5 cm wider than the root ball, and check moisture rather than watering by the calendar.'),
s('Switching materials without surprises','Change one thing at a time. If you move a plant into a new material, a new size and a new mix all at once, you lose any feel for how it will dry. Keep the pot size and mix the same when you swap materials, and relearn the rhythm over two or three waterings.', ['Plastic to terracotta: start checking 2–3 days sooner than you used to.','Terracotta to plastic: stretch the interval, and check that the top 2–5 cm is dry before watering.','Cachepots: empty any water left inside within 15–30 minutes of watering.','Reusing clay pots: scrub off salt crust, soak in 1 part white vinegar to 3 parts water, rinse well.','Disinfect used pots of any material with 1 part household bleach to 9 parts water for 10 minutes, then rinse.'])
],['repotting-houseplants','potting-mix-vs-garden-soil','how-to-water-houseplants']),
article('aroid-potting-mix','Aroid potting mix: a starting recipe and how to tune it','soil-fertilizer','Monstera, philodendron and pothos roots need air as much as water. A 1:1:1 starting blend of potting mix, bark and perlite, and how to adjust it for your pot and light.','Start with roughly 1 part potting mix, 1 part fine bark and 1 part perlite or pumice by volume, then adjust by how fast the pot dries rather than by adding more ingredients.',[
s('Why aroid roots want air','Many of the most popular aroids, including Monstera deliciosa, most climbing Philodendron species, pothos (Epipremnum aureum), Rhaphidophora and Anthurium, are hemiepiphytes or epiphytes. In the wild their roots run over tree bark and through loose leaf litter, where air moves freely and rain drains away within minutes. Oxygen diffuses roughly 10,000 times more slowly through water than through air, so in a dense, saturated peat mix their thick roots suffocate within days, and root-rot organisms such as Pythium move in.\n\nThe aim of a chunky mix is to leave a meaningful share of the pot’s volume, very roughly a fifth to a third, filled with air even right after watering. Not every aroid needs this: peace lily (Spathiphyllum), Aglaonema and Alocasia are terrestrial and prefer a finer, moister mix, while ZZ, also an aroid, wants a gritty, fast-drying one.'),
s('The starting blend and what each part does','This is a starting point, not a formula. Particle size matters more than ingredient names, so look at the bag and feel the texture.', ['1 part quality peat- or coir-based potting mix: holds water and nutrients (peat holds 10–20 times its dry weight in water).','1 part fine orchid or pine bark, 6–20 mm chunks: creates large air pores and breaks down slowly over 2–3 years.','1 part perlite or pumice: permanent pore space. Perlite is lighter and floats to the top; pumice is heavier, holds a little water in its pores and stays put.','Optional: a handful of horticultural charcoal or worm castings. Neither is essential.']),
s('Tune it to your plant, pot and light','Push the coarse fraction (bark plus perlite or pumice) from two-thirds up towards three-quarters of the blend for large pots over 25 cm, plants in medium or low light, cool rooms, velvet-leaved philodendrons such as Philodendron gloriosum, anthuriums, and for growers who water generously. Shift toward more potting mix for small pots under about 12 cm, hot bright windows, pothos in hanging baskets, and terrestrial aroids such as peace lily.\n\nThe pot is part of the equation. Go up only 2–5 cm in diameter at each repotting (5–8 cm for big specimens), and remember that terracotta dries a chunky mix much faster than plastic. For Monstera, adding a moss or coir pole at repotting encourages bigger, more fenestrated leaves as the plant climbs.'),
s('Mix and pot it properly','Moisten peat or coir before mixing, because bone-dry peat repels water, and wet the perlite to keep the fine silica dust down; wear a dust mask for large batches. Combine everything in a tub until it is evenly blended. When potting, do not pack the mix down. Tap the pot on the bench to settle it and let the first watering finish the job.\n\nTease away as much of the old, dense nursery peat from the root ball as the roots allow. If a wet peat core sits inside a fast-draining chunky mix, the two textures do not exchange water well: the outside dries while the core stays soggy. Water thoroughly until 10–20% drains out. Wear gloves, because the sap of these plants contains calcium oxalate crystals that irritate skin and mouths, and all of them are toxic to cats and dogs.'),
s('Read the result and maintain it','A well-tuned mix in bright indirect light lets the top 2–5 cm dry within roughly a week in the growing season. If it stays wet for two weeks or more, smells sour, or lower leaves yellow, it is too dense or the pot is too big. If it dries in two or three days and the plant wilts between waterings, add more potting mix next time.\n\nBark-heavy mixes hold fewer nutrients, and the microbes that slowly decompose bark consume some nitrogen, so feed regularly, every 2–4 weeks, during active growth. As the bark breaks down, the mix slumps and drains more slowly; repot every 1–3 years, ideally in spring or early summer.')
],['monstera-deliciosa-care','perlite-vs-pumice','potting-mix-vs-garden-soil']),
article('self-watering-pots','Self-watering pots: how wicking planters work and which plants suit them','tips','Self-watering planters feed water up from a reservoir by capillary wicking. Learn how they work, which plants and mixes suit them, and how to avoid soggy roots.','Top water for the first 2–4 weeks until roots reach the wicking zone, then let the reservoir run completely dry for a few days between refills so air can return to the roots.',[
s('How the reservoir reaches the roots','A self-watering planter is a small sub-irrigation system. Water sits in a reservoir at the base, separated from the growing mix by a platform or inner pot. A wick, either a fabric cord or a column of mix packed into a perforated basket, reaches down into the water and draws it upward by capillary action, the same force that pulls water up a paper towel. An overflow hole stops the level rising into the root zone, and a float indicator shows how much is left.\n\nThe air gap between the water surface and the bottom of the mix is essential. If the reservoir is overfilled, or the overflow is blocked, the lower mix floods and roots in that zone die. Even when the system works as designed, the mix is wettest at the bottom and driest at the top, and stays moist around the clock.'),
s('Plants that thrive and plants that don’t','Constant moisture is the whole point of these planters, so they suit plants that dislike drying out and punish those that need a dry spell.', ['Good fits: peace lily (Spathiphyllum), spider plant (Chlorophytum comosum), Boston fern, calatheas, and basil or other herbs on a sunny sill.','Workable with care: pothos, monstera and philodendron in bright light, if you let the reservoir run dry for several days between fills.','Poor fits: snake plant, ZZ, aloe, cacti and most succulents. They need the mix to dry through, and constant wetness rots their rhizomes and stem bases.']),
s('The mix matters more than the planter','Wicking needs a continuous network of fine pores. A standard peat- or coir-based potting mix with 20–30% perlite wicks well while keeping some air space. Chunky aroid blends heavy in bark break the capillary chain, so water never reaches the upper roots. Dense, fine mix with no perlite wicks too well and stays close to saturated. Where the planter’s manufacturer specifies a mix, follow it.\n\nNewly potted plants have not yet grown roots down to the wet lower layer. Water them from the top for the first 2–4 weeks, and only then begin filling the reservoir. A float showing “full” tells you about the reservoir, not the root ball; push a finger into the mix to check.'),
s('Salts, gnats and maintenance','In a normal pot, surplus water drains out and carries excess salts with it. A self-watering pot has no drainage path, so minerals from tap water and fertilizer build up, mainly in the top layer, where water evaporates. Every 2–3 months, water heavily from the top, tip the excess out through the overflow and refill with plain water. Feed at a quarter to half the label rate, whether you add it to the reservoir or water it in from the top, and never pour concentrate straight into the reservoir.\n\nThe constantly damp surface is attractive to fungus gnats; a 1–2 cm top dressing of grit helps. Rinse the reservoir whenever it smells or grows algae, and at every repotting.'),
s('Using them for holidays','A self-watering pot can carry a plant through a week or two away, but only if you know how long its reservoir lasts in your conditions. Fill it at home and note how many days it takes to empty; the answer changes with season, light and plant size, so the refill interval on the box is only a guide. Run that test in the same season as your trip, and if the reservoir lasts less than the time you will be away, arrange a sitter visit as well.')
],['vacation-plant-care','how-to-water-houseplants','potting-mix-vs-garden-soil']),
];
export function readingMinutes(a:Pick<Article,'sections'>){return Math.max(2,Math.ceil(a.sections.flatMap(s=>[s.heading,...s.paragraphs,...(s.bullets||[])]).join(' ').split(/\s+/).length/200))}
